        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVE:<BR>
Skeletal muscle is made up of many "motor units."  Each unit consists of a group of muscle fibers and a motor neuron that innervates them.  For each nerve impulse, the muscle fibers perform a single twitch contraction (contract and immediately relax).  This single twitch of a single motor unit could be considered the quantal unit of exercise.  Prolonged weightlessness results in a loss of muscle strength and volume and decreased bone density, particularly in the legs.  At this time, the only effective treatment for these problems appears to be in-flight exercise.  No form of physical activity can be maximally efficient or effective, however, if motor neuron recruitment is compromised.  This may be the case, as previous observations have suggested that Achilles' tendon tap responses and Hoffmann Reflexes in the soleus muscle are abnormal after several days in space.  However, most of these data were obtained days after landing or were incidental observations during experiments designed for other purposes.  The effect of weightlessness on spinal cord excitability has not been fully studied.  The objective of this experiment was to determine if spinal cord excitability gradually decreases during prolonged spaceflight, and if so, to measure the extent of the change, and how long it continues after return to the ground.
<p>
APPROACH:<BR>
During experiment operations, the test subject was fitted with five electrodes.  Electromyography (EMG) activity was recorded differentially from two recording electrodes applied to the soleus muscle.  The locations of these electrodes on the posterior surface of the leg were critical.  A single reference electrode was applied to the anterior surface of the same leg.  The electrical stimulus to the posterior tibial nerve was applied through an adjustable, spring-loaded electrode located behind the knee.   A knee brace provided a rigid and stable structure on which to mount the stimulating electrode.  The stimulus circuit was completed through a stimulus return path electrode positioned just above the knee cap.   Following application of the electrodes, the subject was restrained in a sitting position.  The feet were strapped to a foot brace that established fixed and consistent knee (120 degrees) and ankle (90 degrees) angles that varied somewhat between subjects.  During the “Locate Phase” of the experiment, the subject received electrical shocks (600 microseconds in duration, 40-90 volts amplitude) at the rate of one shock every three seconds.  The purpose of this phase was to allow the operator to locate the posterior tibial nerve (the stimulating electrode could be adjusted for optimum data collection), and determine the shock strength (the electrical shock was varied to find the minimum and maximum values required for the experiment).  No data was stored during the Locate Phase.
<p>
During the “Data Collection Phase” of the experiment, sixty test shocks were delivered at the rate of one every five seconds.  The stimulus was incrementally increased and then slowly decreased within the determined maximum range.  The resulting EMG response from each shock was recorded from the soleus muscle.
<p>
After the last shock was applied, a computer analyzed and displayed the recorded data, allowing the crew member to determine if the test was successfully completed.  At the end of the session, the computer analyzed the data and determined the minimum and maximum of spinal cord excitability for each session.  Subsequently,  the ratio of H-max to M-max (the measure of spinal cord excitability) was determined.    Data was saved on the hard drive and down-linked to ground operations as soon as possible. 
<P>
During flight, H-Reflex recruitment curves were recorded four times during each subjects stay onboard the Station: three times early in flight (pre-docked and docked) and once late in the mission.  The principal investigator added an additional early session during Expedition 3, after results received during Expedition 2 indicated that an additional session would better define the rate of change in this reflex.   For comparison, the experiment was also run three times preflight and four times post-flight.
<p>
RESULTS:<BR>
This study of spinal cord excitability using the Hoffman reflex was completed by a total of eight subjects over ISS Expeditions 2-4.  H-Reflex measured how excitable the nerve cells were by applying small electrical shocks behind the knee.  Each shock produced a reflex response in the calf muscles (the H-Reflex response); the data collected indicated that this response decreased significantly while in microgravity.  The study found that spinal cord excitability decreased by about 35% in weightlessness, and stayed at this new level for the duration of the mission.  Although there was notable improvement in the H-Reflex response the day after landing, it took about ten days back on Earth for astronauts to fully recover their muscle strength and spinal cord excitability.
<P>
This difference in excitability means that only a portion of muscle fiber units are contracting in response to signals from the nervous system and explains functionally why muscle mass declines in weightlessness, even with exercise.  Reduced excitability means that there might be limits on the degree to which heart muscle strength, leg muscle tone, and bone density (for which muscle contraction is an important regulating factor) can be maintained through exercise on long-duration missions.  Because this decrease in excitability is only observed on orbit and not during bed rest, an analogue for weightless space travel, the results highlight the possibility that reduced excitability with corresponding loss of muscle and bone might be partly a nervous system response and not simply due to disuse of the legs. 
<P>
Based on the results of this study, decreased spinal cord excitability could be an issue for long-duration stays in partial-gravity environments such as are found on the moon and Mars.  Future designs of exercise equipment that provide feedback on work actually performed would help crewmembers compensate for decreases in exercise efficiency.
        </div>

